WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.583
Module Ten, Lesson Two: communicating uncertainty,

2
00:00:03.633 --> 00:00:06.117
and designing against automation bias.

3
00:00:06.447 --> 00:00:10.621
An oversight step that people rubber-stamp provides no oversight at all.

4
00:00:11.421 --> 00:00:15.188
Picture a law firm where an AI assistant reviews contracts,

5
00:00:15.238 --> 00:00:19.200
and a paralegal named Sam checks each clause it marks as fine.

6
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:22.232
For months, the assistant is right, again and again.

7
00:00:23.032 --> 00:00:25.457
So Sam stops reading and just clicks accept.

8
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:28.823
Then a risky auto-renewal clause gets a confident all-clear,

9
00:00:28.873 --> 00:00:30.366
and it slips straight through.

10
00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:32.555
That's automation bias:

11
00:00:32.605 --> 00:00:36.620
the tendency to over-trust automated output and stop checking it.

12
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.103
It gets worse as the system gets better, so the fix has to live in the design.

13
00:00:41.903 --> 00:00:44.777
First, show the evidence, not just the conclusion,

14
00:00:44.827 --> 00:00:47.293
so review is real rather than performative.

15
00:00:47.623 --> 00:00:49.019
Then surface uncertainty,

16
00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:53.183
and make low-confidence clauses look different so they demand attention.

17
00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:57.992
Require an active decision, with no pre-selected accept button,

18
00:00:58.042 --> 00:01:00.504
and ask for a brief reason on override.

19
00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:05.669
Rotate and train reviewers on known failure cases, so they stay healthily doubtful.

20
00:01:06.469 --> 00:01:09.683
Then sample-audit the approvals, and measure the override rate.

21
00:01:10.013 --> 00:01:14.631
A rate near zero across thousands of reviews is evidence of rubber-stamping,

22
00:01:14.681 --> 00:01:15.553
not perfection.

23
00:01:16.353 --> 00:01:18.671
Next, communicate confidence honestly.

24
00:01:19.001 --> 00:01:21.716
Raw model probabilities are poorly calibrated,

25
00:01:21.766 --> 00:01:24.241
so never show them as precise percentages.

26
00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:27.206
Better signals are retrieval scores,

27
00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:30.837
whether sources agree, and self-consistency across samples.

28
00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:35.597
Show simple high, medium and low bands, tied to a calibrated threshold.

29
00:01:36.397 --> 00:01:40.367
Language models sound fluent and sure whether they're right or not,

30
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:43.067
so overconfident language is a design defect.

31
00:01:43.397 --> 00:01:45.226
If the assistant can't answer well,

32
00:01:45.276 --> 00:01:48.019
it should say so, and point to someone who can help.

33
00:01:48.819 --> 00:01:53.142
Transparency is both a regulatory expectation and good design.

34
00:01:53.472 --> 00:01:56.710
Tell users they're talking to an AI, cite the sources,

35
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.121
explain its limits, and show a visible route to a human.

36
00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:05.201
For consequential decisions, add a documented appeal and override path.

37
00:02:05.531 --> 00:02:07.866
A person must be able to contest the outcome,

38
00:02:07.916 --> 00:02:10.516
and reach someone with the authority to change it.

39
00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:13.677
Finally, make feedback cheap and specific.

40
00:02:14.007 --> 00:02:16.162
A thumbs-down tells you almost nothing,

41
00:02:16.212 --> 00:02:20.911
but quoted an outdated contract version tells you exactly where the pipeline failed.

42
00:02:21.711 --> 00:02:26.163
Capture each correction against its trace ID, and tell the user what changed.

43
00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:28.483
Then route it into the data flywheel,

44
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:32.288
because human corrections are your best training and evaluation data.

45
00:02:33.088 --> 00:02:36.930
On the exam, a near-zero override rate signals rubber-stamping,

46
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.142
and options that always produce an answer are wrong.

47
00:02:40.472 --> 00:02:43.197
Honest abstention beats a plausible invention,

48
00:02:43.247 --> 00:02:46.033
so read on for the full set of countermeasures.
